<details>
<summary><b>Steps of Reproduction ✅ </b></summary>

```mdx
1. Start the standalone Socket.IO server defined in `server/socket-server.js:1-503`, which
connects to Redis and registers the `disconnect` handler at
`server/socket-server.js:392-438`.

2. From a client integrated with this server, establish a long-lived socket connection and
emit `user-online` once so `trackUserPresence` at `server/socket-server.js:121-126` stores
`user:${socket.id}` with a TTL of 3600 seconds, and join a team so `addUserToRoom` at
`server/socket-server.js:70-77` adds the socket ID to `room:<teamId>`.

3. Keep the socket connection open for longer than one hour so the Redis key
`user:${socket.id}` expires (due to the TTL set in `trackUserPresence`), while the
`room:<teamId>` set still contains the socket ID; then disconnect the client so the
`disconnect` handler at `server/socket-server.js:392-438` runs.

4. During disconnect, `redisClient.hGetAll('user:<socketId>')` at
`server/socket-server.js:398` returns an empty object, causing the `if
(Object.keys(userData).length > 0)` block at `server/socket-server.js:399-411` to be
skipped, but the subsequent loop at `server/socket-server.js:417-433` still emits
`member-left` events with `userId: userData.userId` and `userName: userData.userName`
(both `undefined`), which are then consumed by `handleMemberLeft` in
`hooks/use-socket-client.ts:241-244`, resulting in misleading `member-left` events and
failure to remove the user from the `members` list.
```
