**Suggestion:** The socket initialization effect only sets `socketRef.current` and registers event listeners when `socketInstance` is created for the first hook; any subsequent components calling `useSocket`, `useTeamSocket`, or `useMatchSocket` will see `socket` as `null` and never subscribe to connection events, so their listeners never run even though a global socket connection exists. The effect should always attach listeners and assign `socketRef.current` to the existing `socketInstance`, as well as clean up listeners on unmount, so that each hook instance correctly reflects the global socket's state. [logic error]

<details>
<summary><b>Severity Level:</b> Critical 🚨</summary>

```mdx
- ❌ Additional useSocket callers receive null socket references.
- ⚠️ Secondary hooks misreport connection status despite active socket.
```
</details>

```suggestion
  // Initialize socket
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!session?.user) {
      return;
    }

    // Get token from session (you may need to customize this based on your auth setup)
    const token = (session.user as any).token;

    if (!socketInstance) {
      setIsConnecting(true);
      socketInstance = createSocketConnection(token, options);
    }

    socketRef.current = socketInstance;

    const handleConnect = () => {
      setIsConnected(true);
      setIsConnecting(false);
      setError(null);
    };

    const handleDisconnect = () => {
      setIsConnected(false);
    };

    const handleConnectError = (err: any) => {
      setIsConnected(false);
      setIsConnecting(false);
      setError(err.message);
    };

    socketInstance!.on('connect', handleConnect);
    socketInstance!.on('disconnect', handleDisconnect);
    socketInstance!.on('connect_error', handleConnectError);

    // If the socket is already connected when this hook mounts, sync state
    if (socketInstance!.connected) {
      handleConnect();
    }

    return () => {
      if (socketInstance) {
        socketInstance.off('connect', handleConnect);
        socketInstance.off('disconnect', handleDisconnect);
        socketInstance.off('connect_error', handleConnectError);
      }
    };
  }, [session?.user, options]);
```
<details>
<summary><b>Steps of Reproduction ✅ </b></summary>

```mdx
1. Inspect the global socket hook implementation at `hooks/use-socket-client.ts:79-117`,
where `useSocket()` creates a singleton `socketInstance` and assigns it to
`socketRef.current` only when `!socketInstance && session?.user` (lines 88-95).

2. Note that subsequent `useSocket()` calls in other components (or inside
`useTeamSocket()` / `useMatchSocket()` at `hooks/use-socket-client.ts:185` and `279`) run
the same effect, but now `socketInstance` is already non-null, so the `if (!socketInstance
&& session?.user)` branch is skipped entirely.

3. Because the branch is skipped, those later hook instances never execute
`socketRef.current = socketInstance;` (line 95) and never register their own `connect`,
`disconnect`, or `connect_error` listeners, so their local state (`isConnected`,
`isConnecting`, `error`) remains at initial values and `socket` stays `null`.

4. Following the migration guidance in `DEPLOYMENT.md:105-123` (which recommends importing
`useSocket` / `useTeamSocket` from `hooks/use-socket-client.ts`), any page or component
that uses `useSocket()` in more than one place (e.g., one call directly and one via
`useTeamSocket()`) will reproduce the issue: the first hook instance reflects the real
connection state, while all subsequent instances see `socket: null` and never update their
connection flags.
```
